//? Title: MISER integration
//? Subtitle: Actual pilot budgets, discarded samples and finite replicated errors
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Estimate an integral over a finite box when exact integration is unavailable or
expensive. Recursive stratified sampling uses pilot observations to decide where
to split and how to distribute later evaluations. We inspect the actual sample
accounting, retain the original narrow Gaussian and sixteen-seed comparison, and
distinguish observed accuracy from a universal improvement or error-rate
guarantee.
)

\toc

> Derive the allocation objective

Split a region into equal-volume halves, with volume V/2 each and within-half
integrand standard deviations sigmaL and sigmaR. Independent sample means based
on nL and nR observations have combined variance

$$
\operatorname{Var}(\hat I)=\frac{V^2}{4}\left(\frac{\sigma_L^2}{n_L}+\frac{\sigma_R^2}{n_R}\right).
$$
\alttext(The two half-region sample means contribute their variance divided by their respective sample counts.)

For fixed nL+nR=N, differentiation gives nL/nR=sigmaL/sigmaR. The ideal minimum
is V squared times(sigmaL+sigmaR) squared divided by4N, motivating the split
axis with the smallest estimated sum of half-region standard deviations. Unknown
deviations require pilots; finite pilot noise, minimum allocations, rounding and
discarded pilot cost all separate the implementation from this ideal
calculation. It does not prove faster asymptotic convergence than ordinary Monte
Carlo.

The native variant uses10percent pilots, at least16 observations, whenever a
region budget is at least64. It discards those observations from the final
estimator, allocates the remainder proportionally, then recurses. Budgets
below64 use plain leaves. The legacy32-observation allocation clamps are
retained, so small split budgets64..79 can produce one leaf smaller than32.
Successful execution nevertheless makes exactly the requested number of
callbacks, with every leaf having at least two.

> A constant integrand and an actual split

Integrating the constant2 over[0,1] gives2 exactly and zero plug-in variance.
With128 calls, the actual root pilot uses16 and both children receive56. The
diagram reads those actual allocations. All128 samples are stored with their
true pilot/leaf labels; no report-side simulation is needed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/miser_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::miser_trace([](const auto &) {
        return 2.;
    }, {0}, {1}, 128);
    auto &n = t.nodes.front();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("root", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual root budget {}\npilot {}", n.calls, n.pilot_calls)).node("left", {7, -2}, std::format("Actual left budget\n{}", n.left_calls)).node("right", {7, 2}, std::format("Actual right budget\n{}", n.right_calls)).edge("root", "left").edge("root", "right");
    std::print("Actual estimate: {}, native variance: {}, evaluations: {}.\n\n| node | calls | pilot calls " "| left calls | right calls | returned integral |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: " "|\n",
               t.result.value, t.result.variance, t.evaluations);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.nodes.size(); ++i) {
        auto &r = t.nodes[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, r.calls,
                   r.pilot_calls, r.left_calls, r.right_calls, r.result.value);
    }
    d.save_svg("build/miser_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Actual estimate: 2, native variance: 0, evaluations: 128.

| node | calls | pilot calls | left calls | right calls | returned integral |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 128 | 16 | 56 | 56 | 2 |
| 1 | 56 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 |
| 2 | 56 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 |
\image(figures/miser_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual pilot and child budgets for a constant integral.)
\alttext(The root spends16 pilot calls and distributes the remaining112 equally between two56-call leaves.)

> Retain the original narrow Gaussian

The original integrand is exp(−200 times((x−0.5) squared+(y−0.5) squared)) on
the unit square. Separating coordinates gives the exact finite-box integral
pi/200 times erf(sqrt(50)) squared. The original pi/200 reference was the
infinite-plane approximation; here the finite-box correction is explicit, though
negligible at ordinary double precision.

The original main call uses200000 evaluations and seed1. A smaller2000-call
trace reveals actual pilot and leaf positions without burdening the figure with
all200000 points. The plot is about sampling placement, not an independent
replication of the main estimate. The native fixture and comparison logic live
in the core.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/miser_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r =
        dm::algorithms::miser_integrate(dm::algorithms::miser_gaussian, {0, 0},
                                        {1, 1}, 200000, 1);
    auto t =
        dm::algorithms::miser_trace(dm::algorithms::miser_gaussian, {0, 0},
                                    {1, 1}, 2000, 1);
    std::vector<double> px, py, lx, ly;
    for (auto &s : t.samples)
        if (s.pilot) {
            px.push_back(s.point[0]);
    py.push_back(s.point[1]);
        } else {
            lx.push_back(s.point[0]);
            ly.push_back(s.point[1]);
        }
        std::print("| original calls | native estimate | exact finite-box reference | absolute error | native " "variance estimate |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 200000 | {} | {} | {} | {} " "|\n\nFigure trace evaluations: {}.\n",
                   r.value, dm::algorithms::miser_gaussian_integral(),
                   std::abs(r.value - dm::algorithms::miser_gaussian_integral()),
                   r.variance, t.evaluations);
        auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
        p.points(lx, ly, "retained leaf samples", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(
            px, py, "discarded pilot samples", {220, 38, 38}, 2).size(850, 850).margins(95, 35, 60, 70).x_limits(0, 1).y_limits(0, 1).title("Original Gaussian: actual 2000-call sampling trace").x_label("x").y_label("y").save_svg("build/miser_original.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| original calls | native estimate | exact finite-box reference | absolute error | native variance estimate |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 200000 | 0.01573267353462269 | 0.015707963267948967 | 2.4710266673722003e-05 | 1.5056400623214882e-09 |

Figure trace evaluations: 2000.
